 { Black has an extra pawn, but White is reasonably active. Smyslov found a great solution, based on the use of his king as an attacking piece. } 1... { -KEY- } Kh6 $1 { Heading for the weak enemy pawns at h3 and f3, without worrying about the f7-pawn. } ( { In contrast, } 1... Kf8 $6 { would be a step in the wrong direction, when } 2. Kb3 Ke7 3. Ne4 { preparing a3-a4, yields White some counterplay. } ) 
2. Ne4 ( { Or } 2. Nxf7 Kh5 { with the strong plan of ...Kh4xh3. } ) 
Kh5 $1 { In the same spirit of activity above material. } 3. Nxf6+ Kg5 4. Nxh7+ Kxf5 5. h4 Be3 ( { With the threat of } 6... Bh6 { , trapping the knight. } ) 
6. Nf8 { The sealed move, although White resigned without resuming. Let's see a possible continuation: } Kf4 7. Nd7 Bd4 8. Nf6 ( { After } 8. h5 Kxf3 9. Kd3 f5 10. h6 Ba1 11. h7 e4 { the connected passed pawns decide - Smyslov. } ) 
Kxf3 9. Kd3 Kf4 10. Nd5+ Kg4 11. Nc7 f5 12. Nxa6 f4 13. Nc7 f3 14. Nxb5 Kh3 $1 { The f-pawn soon queens - Smyslov. } 
 0-1

