 { White has some initiative thanks to his control of the open file, though the passive state of his minor pieces makes it difficult to capitalize on it at this moment. Onischuk instead opts for a useful move, while awaiting the opponent's next step. } 1. { -KEY- } Kf2 $1 { Approaching with the king is rarely in vain when the endgame is around the corner. } dxe4 $6 { Malakhov prepares a little positional combination, but we will soon see its defects. } ( { Onischuk instead suggests } 1... Kf8 $5 { copying White's idea. } ) 
2. Qxb7 ( { After } 2. fxe4 Qa7 { Black would have some counterplay against the enemy pawn centre. } ) 
e3+ { The point: White is left with an isolated pawn on d4. } ( { Instead, after } 2... Rxb7 3. fxe4 { Black is clearly worse - Onischuk; even so, I suspect that this was the least of his evils. } ) 
3. Kxe3 Rxb7 { In structural terms Black is fine here, but note the difference in activity between the two kings! With his following moves Onischuk exploits this factor in energetic fashion. } 4. Rc8 $1 Rb8 ( { Or } 4... Kf8 5. d5 $1 exd5 6. Bxg7 Kxg7 7. Rxe8 { with a clear advantage - Onischuk. } ) 
5. Rxb8 Bxb8 6. d5 $1 { A key move that clears a path for the king. In the absence of rooks, Black won't be able to resist the invasion of this piece. } ( { Less was promised by } 6. Kd3 Bd6 { preparing }  Nc7 { with a barrier. } ) 
e5 ( { After } 6... exd5 7. Kd4 Nc7 8. Kc5 $1 Kf8 9. Kb6 { Black's task is just as hopeless. } ) 
7. Kd3 $1 Kf8 ( { Or } 7... Bd6 8. Kc4 Nc7 9. Ng3 $1  { with a crushing } 10. Ne4 { next. } ) 
8. Kc4 Ke7 9. Kb5 Bd6 10. Kc6 $1 { Restricting the enemy plan of ...Nc7 and ...Kd7, while situating the king at the 'magic' distance from the black knight. } ( 10. Kxa5 $6 Nc7 { was less technical. } ) 
g6 ( 10... g6 { prepares to meet } 11. Ng3 { by } f5 { but now White hurries to improve his bishop instead. } ) 
11. Bc1 $1 f5 ( { a) Just as bad is } 11... Nf6 12. Bg5 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 11... Kd8 12. Be3 { heading for b6. } ) 
12. Bg5+ Kf7 13. Bd8 e4 14. fxe4 fxe4 15. Bxa5 Ke7 16. Bb6 g5 17. Nd4 { In this hopeless position, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

