[Result "1-0"]
[FEN "n5k1/4nppp/2p1p3/1pPp2P1/1P1P2B1/8/3BPP1P/6K1 w - - 0 1"]

 { As in any endgame the pair of bishops is an asset, but the closed pawn structure helps the knights to some extent. } 1. { -KEY- } Bf4 { Before anything else, it makes sense to restrict the knight on a8. } Kf8 2. Kg2 { Both sides hurry to bring their kings into the battle. } Ke8 3. Be5 g6 $6 ( { This was a good moment for active defence with } 3... Kd7 $1 4. Bxg7 Nc7 { followed by ...Na6xb4 - Miles. } ) 
4. Kf3 Kd7 5. Kf4 Nc7 $2 { Black's play is very consistent, but he forgets about the opponent's possibilities. } ( 5... Ng8 { first was correct, when } 6. h4 Nc7 7. Bxc7 $1 Kxc7 8. Ke5 Kd7 9. h5 { keeps some winning chances - Miles. } ) 
6. Bxc7 $1 { This exchange doesn't just eliminate the ...Na6 possibility, it also opens a further path for White's king. } Kxc7 7. Ke5 Ng8 { Black seems to have everything under control; however... } 8. Bxe6 $1 { A great sacrifice that opens the gates for White's king. } ( 8. h4 { was a slower option, transposing to 5...Ng8 above. } ) 
fxe6 9. Kxe6 Kd8 10. Kd6 $1 ( { Not } 10. Kf7 $6 Ne7 { With the text White ties the knight to the c6-pawn, and the enemy king to the knight. } ) 
 ( 10. f3 $5 { preparing e2-e4 is another good option, as observed by UltraSuperBrainPower. We'll see that Miles resorts to this idea a few moves later in the game. } ) 
Ne7 11. e3 { Prophylaxis against ...Nf5 and, at the same time, intending to drive away Black's king from the key square on d7. } Ke8 12. Kc7 Kf7 13. Kd7 Kf8 14. f3 $1 { Creating a passed pawn, a plan that Black can't parry in any way. } Nf5 ( { Or } 14... Kf7 15. e4 dxe4 16. fxe4 Kf8 17. h3 $1 Kf7 18. e5 Kf8 19. e6 { (zugzwang) } Nd5 20. Kxc6 Nxb4 21. Kxb5 { and wins - Miles. } ) 
15. Kxc6 Nxe3 16. Kd7 $1 { Back to the key square on d7. White's king is a real monster in this example. } Nc4 17. c6 Nb6+ 18. Kd8 ( { In view of } 18. Kd8 Kf7 19. c7 Ke6 20. c8=Q Nxc8 21. Kxc8 Kf5 22. Kd7 { followed by Kd6xd5, Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

