 { Sometimes the endgame phase arrives earlier than expected, for example when one of the sides hasn't yet castled. On such occasions it is worth considering the idea of keeping the king in the centre, thus saving a few tempi on its activation - compare the g1-g2-g3-f4 and g8-f8-e8-d7 routes used for this purpose in Miles-Gonzalez earlier in this section. Here is one related case.The battle has reached the endgame, so Rozentalis keeps his king in the centre. } 1... { -KEY- } Ke7 $1 { From now on, the difference in activity between the two kings will be an important factor, just like in Example 471 (Onischuk-Malakhov). } 2. b4 ( { a) If } 2. e5 { , then } Rac8 3. Rac1 b6 { - Rozentalis, when Black is a bit more comfortable - and note that the bishop is not very powerful in this particular pawn structure. } ) 
 ( { b) But the simple } 2. Rfd1 Rhd8 3. Kf1 { might have been preferable, avoiding further weaknesses while activating the king. } ) 
Nd3 3. Be2 Rhd8 4. Rfd1 Ne5 $1 ( { Not } 4... Nxb4 $6 5. Rdb1 { , when White regains the pawn with increased activity. } ) 
5. a3 Rxd1+ 6. Rxd1 Rc8 { Now the absence of White's king from the battle is strongly felt, whereas Black's is doing an important job in denying the enemy rook the d6- and d7-squares. } 7. f4 { The threat of 7...Rc3 forces White to play actively. } Rc2 $1 { A strong intermediate move which definitely hands over the initiative to Black. } ( { In contrast, after } 7... Nc6 $6 8. Kf2 { , followed by Ke3, White manages to activate his king with roughly equal play. } ) 
8. fxe5 { McNab tries his luck in a rook ending. } ( { a) } 8. Bb5 Nf3 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 8. Kf2 Nc4 { was less challenging for Black - Rozentalis. } ) 
Rxe2 9. Rc1 Kd7 10. Rf1 Ke8 { By means of a little king maneuver, Rozentalis avoids Rc7 coming with check on the 12th move. } 11. Rc1 Re3 $1 { The a3-pawn is more important than the doubled ones on the e-file, which can be picked up later. } ( { After } 11... Rxe4 $6 12. Rc8 Kd7 13. Rf8 Ke7 14. Rb8 { , White's counterplay should suffice for a draw. } ) 
12. Kf2 Rxa3 13. Rc8+ Kd7 14. Rf8 Ke7 15. Rb8 g5 $1 { The key move of this endgame, which creates a route for the king via f8-g7-g6 and at the same time fixes White's kingside pawns. } 16. Rxb7+ Kf8 17. Rb8+ Kg7 18. Ra8 h5 19. h4 ( 19. b5 Ra5 20. Ke3 Rxb5 21. Rxa7 Rxe5 22. Kf3 { was more tenacious, although after } Rb5 23. Ra3 Kf6 24. Rc3 Rb2 { , the threat of 25...Rh2 forces White to weaken himself by h3-h4 or g3-g4 anyway - Rozentalis. } ) 
g4 $1 { Avoiding pawn exchanges, while fixing weak points on g3 and f3. } 20. b5 Rf3+ 21. Ke2 Rb3 22. Rxa7 Rxb5 23. Ke3 Rb3+ $1 { Before capitalizing on the doubled e-pawns, Black aims to displace the enemy king. } 24. Kf2 ( { Or } 24. Kf4 Rf3 $1 ( { but not } 24. Kf2 Kg6 $2 25. Ra3 $1 Rb2 26. Rd3 { - Rozentalis } ) 
25. Kg5 Rxg3 26. Kxh5 Re3 27. Kxg4 Rxe4 28. Kg3 Rxe5 { with a type of rook ending that is usually won in practice, thanks to the connected passed pawns. } ) 
Rb2+ 25. Kf1 ( { After } 25. Ke3 Rg2 26. Kf4 Kg6 { , the threat of ...Rf2-f3 decides - Rozentalis. } ) 
Rb4 26. Kf2 Rxe4 27. Ra5 { At first sight it is not evident how to progress with Black, but Rozentalis soon finds the winning plan: a rook transfer to f5. } Kg6 28. Rb5 Ra4 ( 28... Kf5 29. Rb7 { is less productive. } ) 
29. Rc5 Ra2+ $1 { A typical intermediate check in rook endgames, so that ...Ra3 comes with tempo on the next move. } 30. Kf1 Ra3 31. Kg2 Rf3 $1 32. Rc7 ( { Or } 32. Rc2 Rf5 33. Re2 Kg7 { and a king march to d5 decides - Rozentalis. } ) 
Rf5 33. Rc5 f6 { White resigned as he will lose a second pawn. } 
 0-1

