 { Here is a tense endgame which is difficult to assess immediately. White's pressure at g7 restricts the enemy pieces to a considerable extent, but Black controls the open d-file and, in the long run, his knight is superior to the bishop in this structure. Seeing that White probably aims to increase the pressure by Rf2-g2 and g4-g5, Korchnoi hurries to launch a counterattack on the opposite flank... with the king alone! } 1... { -KEY- } Kc7 $1 { The first step of a long journey. It also makes tactical sense to remove the king from the two back ranks, in order to avoid any pins. } ( { a) Less productive was } 1... Rd2 $6 2. Rf2 ) 
 ( { b) Though } 1... Nh6 $5 { heading for f7-g5, was also interesting. } ) 
2. Rf2 ( { An alternative was } 2. a4 $5 { in order to clear the a-file for the rook, thus preventing the further course of the game. There could follow } Kb6 ( { not } 2. Rf2 bxa4 $6 3. Ra1 ) 
3. axb5 axb5 4. Ra1 Rd3 $5 5. Be2 $1 Re3 6. Kf1 Rd2 7. Rxg7 $1 { when both captures on e2 permit 8 Raa7! with a perpetual along the seventh rank. } ) 
Kb6 $1 { By now Black's intentions are clear: ... Ka5-a4-b3 attacking the enemy pawns. } 3. Rg2 Ka5 4. g5 Ka4 $1 { Korchnoi sacrifices a pawn so as to speed up his 'king's attack'. } 5. gxf6 Nxf6 6. Rhxg7 Rxg7 7. Rxg7 Kb3 ( { a) Of course not } 7... Rg8 $2 8. Bd1 $1 { and the king would have to retreat. } ) 
 ( { b) After } 7... Kb3 { in contrast, both } 8. Rg6 $2 ( { and } 7... Kb3 7. Rg2 $2 { run into } Rg8 $1 { with a hopeless minor piece ending for White. However, Hernandez has a much better move prepared. } ) 
 ) 
8. Re7 $1 { The e5-pawn is the key to White's potential success. } Kxb2 $1 { Again Korchnoi plays with maximum consequence. } ( { After } 8... Nd7 $6 9. Re6 Kxb2 10. Rxa6 Kxc3 11. Rd6 $1 { White achieves unnecessary counterplay. } ) 
9. Rxe5 Kxc3 10. Re6 Nd7 11. Rxa6 $2 { That pawn will cost White dearly. } ( { a) After } 11. f6 Kb2 12. Rd6 c3 13. Bd1 Rg8 14. Kf1 ( { or } 14. Kf2 $2 Nxf6 $1 15. Rxf6 Rd8 16. Ke1 Rxd1 $1 ) 
Nxf6 $1 15. Rxf6 Rd8 16. Rf2 Kxa3 17. Ke2 b4 { Black should also win. } ) 
 ( { b) Instead, the restrictive } 11. Rd6 $1 { was worth a shot; e.g. } Kb2 ( { perhaps Black should try } 11. Rxa6 Rg8 $5 12. Kf2 Ne5 { in similar spirit to the game, though } 13. Be2 { sees White much better co-ordinated than after the text; } ) 
12. Kf2 c3 13. Bd1 Kxa3 14. f6 { and the battle is far from over. } ) 
Ne5 $1 { A move with several virtues: the enemy passed pawns are blockaded, the d-file is cleared for the rook, the white rook is deprived of the c6-square, everything with a gain of tempo. } 12. Kf2 $2 { A natural move, but tactically inadequate due to Black's checking possibility on d3. } ( { a) Also bad is } 12. Be2 $2 { in view of } Kd2 13. Re6 Kxe2 14. Rxe5 Rc8 $1 { and the passed pawn decides; e.g. } 15. Rxb5 c3 16. f6 c2 17. Rb2 Ke3 18. Rxc2 Rxc2 19. e5 Rf2 { followed by ... Rf5xe5-e8. } ) 
 ( { b) But } 12. Bg2 { was possible, hiding the bishop from Black's king. After } Kd2 13. Re6 Nd3 14. Bf1 $1 { White aims to swap the minor pieces and then try to save himself in a rook vs. pawns ending. Nevertheless, I think that Black wins by precise play. Here is one sample line: } c3 15. Bxd3 Rxd3 16. Rc6 c2 17. Kf2 Rc3 $1 18. Rd6 Kc1 19. Rh6 Kb2 20. Rh1 Rd3 $1 21. Ke2 Rd4 $1 22. Ke3 Rd1 23. Rh2 Rf1 $1 { Black has managed the best angle for his rook - behind the passed pawns. There can follow } 24. Rxc2 ( { or } 24. Kd4 Kb1 25. Rxc2 Kxc2 26. Kc5 Kd3 $1 27. Kxb5 Kxe4 28. a4 Kd5 $1 { in 'king first!' spirit; } ) 
Kxc2 25. Ke2 Rd1 $1 26. Ke3 ( { both } 26. e5 Rd5 ) 
 ( { and } 26. f6 Rd6 27. e5 Re6 { fail for White; } ) 
Kc3 27. f6 Re1 28. Kf4 Kd4 29. Kf5 Rxe4 30. f7 Re1 31. Kg6 Rf1 32. Kg7 Kc3 { and wins. } ) 
